  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 AM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor satellite depicts mid to high level moisture \nstreaming in from the eastern Pacific, over the Rockies and into the \nGreat Plains as a positively tilted upper trough digs down the \nwestern US coast. As the upper ridging is broken down and shoved \neast, the moisture being injected aloft is aligning with lee \ncyclogenesis over SD. An MCS is blossoming this morning, and will \ncontinue to travel east, around the upper ridge. The upper troughing \nwill slowly track over the northern Rockies this weekend, however \nbefore this the surface low will drift towards the western Great \nLakes region. This will gradually lift the warm sector towards MI \n(starting today). Cloud cover will increase as intermittent light \nrain begins later this afternoon. Lightly breezy southwest winds \nwill become breezy this afternoon (especailly over NW lower), with \noccasional gusts up to 15 to 25 mph (strongest over coastal and \ncoastal adjacent areas).  \n\nBy Sunday morning, the warm surface front will stall somewhere over \nthe CWA. By this time, the deeper moisture will have officially \narrived, and with it a moderately unstable airmass (around 1000 j/kg) \nand healthy shear (25 to 30 kts sfc to 3km). Embedded showers and \nstorms will have a chance to form right along the boundary \nthroughout the day, however will likely struggle to form elsewhere \nover the CWA. That is not to say established showers and storms \ncouldn't move into northern MI from across the lake. This \nenvironment will be capable of training storms, which is apparent in \nmodeled QPF signals where a majority show one main +1\" swath \nsomewhere over northern MI by Monday morning. Higher uncertainty lies \nin the exact location (due to where the warm front stalls) and exact \ntiming (Sunday afternoon or overnight). The potential of training \nshowers and storms results in concerns for flooding. Due to the \nlimited coverage of highest amounts, flooding is expected to remain \nisolated and mostly local (low spots in hilly terrain and a brief \nrise in creeks and streams). \n\nWith the very moist airmass and healthy lower level shear profiles \n(especailly true along the boundary itself), there will be a non-\nzero chance for tornados. Gusty outflow winds are also possible, \nhowever it will be hard for large hail to reach the surface without \nmelting on its way down. To summarize, severe threats with storms is \nnot expected at this time. \n\nEarly next week, the upper level trough becomes negatively tilted and \nwraps up over the Dakotas. Warmer temperatuers will continue during \nthis time, as the axis of this weaker upper ridge lands over MI \nMonday. Moisture will linger, which continues intermittent chances \nfor showers and storms (especially as the warm front transitions \ninto a stationary boundary and could be contaminated by cold pools. \nShowers and storms could fire off of smaller, more mesoscale \nboundaries Monday. Due to the overall weaker forcing and \nenvironment, coverage is not expected to be widespread and \nintensity is expected to be more minimal. The upper closed low will \ncontinue east, tracking along the international border and reaching \nnorthern MI by late Tuesday. Some guidance wants to drag a dry slot \nalong with this (on the southern side of the upper closed low). It \nis too early to have a good read on the chances for this, however it \ncould pause rain chances for Tuesday. \n\nAn active pattern over the northern Rockies will continue into early \nSeptember, which will continue to transport Pacific mid to high \nlevel moisture. This could be supplemented by lower level Gulf \nmoisture a few times, which would continue the on and off chances \nfor precipitation through the end of the work week.",
